## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime-extensible backends using patterns beyond a single factory, with practical concerns like data transfer and policy-based selection.

## Key Concepts
- **Strategy**: Choose a backend at runtime based on plan characteristics
- **Registry**: Register backends by name and instantiate lazily
- **Abstraction**: Small Backend interface minimizes ground-truth coupling
- **Safety**: Backends declare capability and isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
interface Plan { /* ... */ }
interface ExecContext { /* ... */ }
interface ExecutionResult { rows: any[]; metadata?: Record<string, unknown>; }

interface Backend {
  supports(plan: Plan): boolean;
  execute(plan: Plan, ctx: ExecContext): Promise<ExecutionResult>;
}

type BackendFactory = () => Backend;

class BackendRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, BackendFactory>();
  private instances = new Map<string, Backend>();

  register(name: string, factory: BackendFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  get(name: string): Backend | null {
    if (!this.instances.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name);
      if (!factory) return null;
      this.instances.set(name, factory());
    }
    return this.instances.get(name)!;
  }

  listAvailable(): string[] {
    return Array.from(this.factories.keys());
  }
}

class QueryPlanner {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry) {}

  private selectBackend(plan: Plan): Backend | null {
    const available = this.registry.listAvailable();

    // Policy: prefer GPU for vectorizable plans, fallback to CPU
    if (available.includes('gpu')) {
      const gpu = this.registry.get('gpu');
      if (gpu?.supports(plan)) return gpu;
    }

    if (available.includes('fpga')) {
      const fpga = this.registry.get('fpga');
      if (fpga?.supports(plan)) return fpga;
    }

    // Default fallback
    if (available.includes('cpu')) {
      return this.registry.get('cpu');
    }

    return null;
  }

  async execute(plan: Plan, ctx: ExecContext): Promise<ExecutionResult> {
    const backend = this.selectBackend(plan);
    if (!backend) throw new Error('No suitable backend found');
    return backend.execute(plan, ctx);
  }
}

// Usage example
const registry = new BackendRegistry();

// Register backends at runtime
registry.register('cpu', () => new CPUBackend());
registry.register('gpu', () => new GPUBackend());
registry.register('fpga', () => new FPGABackend());

const planner = new QueryPlanner(registry);

const plan: Plan = { type: 'vector_scan', data: [1,2,3,4] };
const ctx: ExecContext = { sessionId: 'abc123' };

// Planner picks optimal backend based on policy
const result = await planner.execute(plan, ctx);
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests knowledge of runtime extensibility, per-endpoint configuration, and safe concurrency control in distributed systems. The candidate must justify pattern choices, discuss trade-offs in dynamic environments, and demonstrate awareness of deadlock prevention and backpressure strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for encapsulating concurrency behavior
- Registry + Factory pattern for runtime-extensible policy injection
- Per-endpoint isolation with sensible defaults
- Deadlock avoidance through careful resource acquisition ordering and bounded queues
- Backpressure considerations to prevent memory exhaustion under load

## Code Example
```typescript
interface ConcurrencyPolicy {
  submit(job: () => void | Promise<void>): void;
  start(): void;
  stop(): void;
}

interface PolicyFactory {
  create(config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy;
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, PolicyFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: PolicyFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string, config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy {
    const factory = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown policy: ${name}`);
    return factory.create(config);
  }
}

// Built-in policies
class FixedWorkerPool implements ConcurrencyPolicy {
  private queue: Array<() => void> = [];
  private active = 0;

  constructor(private maxWorkers: number) {}

  submit(job: () => void): void {
    if (this.active < this.maxWorkers) {
      this.active++;
      job();
    } else {
      this.queue.push(job);
    }
  }

  start(): void { /* drain queue */ }
  stop(): void { /* reject queued */ }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();
registry.register('fixed', (config) => new FixedWorkerPool(config?.workers ?? 10));

const runner = new TaskRunner(registry);
runner.assign('POST /orders', 'fixed', { workers: 20 });
```

## Trade-off Discussion
| Aspect | FixedWorkerPool | WorkStealingPool | BandwidthLimitedPool |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|----------------------|
| Throughput | Predictable, bounded | High under skew | Rate-limited |
| Latency | Moderate queue delay | Low for varied work | Controlled but variable |
| Complexity | Low | High (deques, synchronization) | Medium (token bucket) |
| Deadlock Risk | Low if bounded queue | Medium (circular stealing) | Low |
| Memory | Bounded by queue size | Unbounded steals possible | Bounded by token rate |

